course: chemistry
lesson: 11
title: The candle and what burning is
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: A candle looks simple, but it is a little chemistry factory: the flame melts the wax, the liquid wax climbs the wick, turns into gas, and the gas is what actually burns.
explain: Burning means a fuel joining with oxygen from the air and giving out heat and light; the wax becomes carbon dioxide and water vapour, which is why the candle gets shorter.
explain: Put a jar over the candle and the flame soon dies, because it cannot get fresh oxygen.
explain: Your body does the same reaction slowly and gently: it "burns" food with oxygen to keep you warm and moving, with no flame at all.
explain: A grown-up can show the parts of a flame, but flames are never a toy.
fact: In a candle it is the wax gas that burns, not the solid wax or the string; a freshly blown-out candle can relight through its smoke.
fact: A candle flame is blue at the bottom where it gets plenty of air, bright yellow in the middle where specks of soot glow, and darker at the tip.
fact: A gas cooker flame is blue because air is mixed with the gas before it burns.
fact: A jar over a candle makes the flame go out because it runs out of oxygen; a bigger jar holds more air, so the candle lasts longer.
fact: The water that rises in a jar over a candle in water is not only because oxygen is used up; the air in the jar also cools and shrinks.
fact: Burning wax joins its carbon and hydrogen with oxygen and makes carbon dioxide and water vapour.
fact: A cold plate held above a flame gets black soot: unburnt carbon from the yellow part of the flame; soot was once used to make black ink.
fact: Breathing and burning are the same kind of reaction: food or fuel joins with oxygen, giving energy, carbon dioxide and water.
fact: Trick birthday candles have a metal powder, magnesium, in the wick that catches fire again from the glowing wick.
fact: Striking a match makes heat by friction; safety matches light only on the box strip, which holds red phosphorus.
fact: Wood gives off gases that burn with flames; charcoal has already had its gases driven off, so it glows instead.
fact: Burning charcoal gives off carbon monoxide, an invisible poisonous gas, so barbecues must never be brought indoors or into a tent.
fact: An ordinary metal spoon does not burn in a flame, it only gets hot; very fine steel wool can burn because thin threads have so much surface.
exercise: What actually burns in a candle flame? | Wax that has turned into gas | The cotton string of the wick | The solid wax at the very bottom | The air just above the candle
exercise: Why is a candle flame blue at the bottom and yellow in the middle? | Blue burns cleanly with plenty of air, yellow is glowing soot | The blue part is the coldest and the yellow part is wet with melted wax from below | The wick is blue and the wax is yellow | Blue is smoke and yellow is light from the room
exercise: A glass jar is put over a burning candle. What happens? | The flame shrinks and goes out | The flame gets bigger and brighter | The flame turns blue and stays lit | Nothing, the candle burns on as before
exercise: Where does the wax go as a candle gets shorter? | Into the air as carbon dioxide and water vapour | Down into the wood of the table | Into the wick, which gets fatter | Nowhere, it hides inside the candle
exercise: What is the black smudge on a plate held above a flame? | Soot, unburnt carbon from the flame | Paint from the plate, burnt off by the heat | Grey ash that comes from the melted wax | Dust from the room drawn up by the heat
exercise: How is burning like breathing? | Both join fuel with oxygen, giving energy, carbon dioxide and water | Both need a small flame inside | Both make oxygen | Both happen only in daylight
exercise: Why does a trick birthday candle relight by itself? | Metal powder in the wick catches fire from the glowing wick | It has a tiny battery and a spark maker hidden inside the wax at the bottom | Your breath feeds it more gas | It is filled with helium
exercise: What makes a match light when you strike it? | Friction makes heat that sets off the chemicals in the head | The box has a tiny spark plug hidden inside the brown strip on its side | Air rushes into the match | Sunlight stored in the wood
exercise: Why does wood burn with flames but charcoal just glows? | Charcoal's gases have already been driven off | Charcoal is still wet deep inside, so it cannot flame | Wood is made of metal | Charcoal has no carbon in it
exercise: Why doesn't a metal spoon catch fire in a flame? | Its metal does not react easily with oxygen in a flame | Spoons are always too wet from washing to catch fire | The flame is scared of shiny things | Spoons are filled with water
exercise: Why is a gas cooker flame blue? | Air is mixed with the gas before it burns | The gas pipes are painted blue on the inside | Gas is a blue liquid | The pan reflects blue light
exercise: Why are they called "safety" matches? | They light only on the strip on the box | They cannot burn at all, even in a fire | They go out by themselves in a second | They are made of damp wood
exercise: Why is a barbecue never brought into a tent? | It gives off carbon monoxide, an invisible poison | It makes the tent much too bright for sleeping | Charcoal stops glowing in tents | The smoke smells of sausages
exercise: Why does a bigger jar keep a candle alight longer? | It holds more air, so more oxygen | It is much colder inside | It has thicker glass | The flame is less frightened
test: Your friend blows out a candle, a grown-up brings a match to the smoke, and the candle relights. Why? | The smoke still holds wax gas, which catches fire | The match touched the wick by accident | Smoke is made of pure oxygen, which bursts into flame | The candle was never really out
test: The class video says: "The water rises in the jar because exactly the oxygen is used up." What is missing? | The air also cools and shrinks after the flame goes out | Nothing, that is the whole story and nothing else happens in the jar | The water is sucked up by the wick | The jar gets lighter and floats
test: "why don't gas hobs leave black crap on pans but candles do on plates" Best answer? | Gas mixed with air burns cleanly with no soot | Gas hobs are wiped clean by tiny robots | Pans are too hot for soot | Candle wax is black inside
test: Why are you warm? | Your body slowly burns food with oxygen, giving out heat | Your blood is heated by the Sun every day, even through the clouds | Your clothes make heat | Your bones are always hot
test: In a cartoon, a camper brings a glowing barbecue into the tent "to keep warm". What's wrong? | Charcoal gives off carbon monoxide, which can kill silently | Nothing, it is a great way to stay warm on a cold night in the woods | The tent would get too cold | The charcoal would go out at once
test: Your brother says a candle gets shorter because the wax "just melts and drips". What is the truth? | Most of the wax burns into carbon dioxide and water vapour | All of it melts and drips down onto the table, where you can scrape it off | It sinks into the wick | It turns back into a solid inside
test: What would happen to a candle under a very small jar? | It would go out very quickly | It would burn on forever and ever | It would grow taller and brighter | It would turn blue and stay lit
test: "steel wool burns but my spoon doesn't, they're both steel, wtf" Best answer? | Thin threads have lots of surface for oxygen to reach | Steel wool is not made of metal at all, it is a kind of sheep's wool | Spoons are fireproof plastic | Only spoons are coated with ice
test: A grown-up holds a cold spoon high above a candle and drops of water appear. Where do they come from? | They are made by the burning of the wax | They leak out through the metal of the spoon | They come out of the cotton wick as it burns | They are drops of dew from the night air
test: Your little cousin asks how a trick candle "knows" to relight. Best answer? | It doesn't know; hot metal powder in the wick catches fire again | It has a tiny brain in the wax that tells it when the party is not over | It listens for clapping | It is magic
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the combustion of a candle? | The vaporised wax combines with oxygen, yielding carbon dioxide and water vapour | The cotton wick alone is consumed, the wax remaining wholly intact and merely dripping | The solid wax combines directly with nitrogen | The flame destroys the atoms of the wax
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a candle enclosed beneath a jar is extinguished. | The supply of oxygen within the jar is insufficient to sustain combustion | The glass of the jar absorbs the heat of the flame | The wax is exhausted at the moment of enclosure, the jar drawing it upward | Light cannot pass through the jar
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning respiration and combustion. | Both combine carbon-based substances with oxygen, liberating energy | Respiration produces oxygen, whereas combustion consumes it entirely | Combustion requires no oxygen whatsoever | Respiration requires an open flame within the body
exam: It is given that a charcoal barbecue has been introduced into an enclosed tent. Ascertain the principal hazard. | The accumulation of carbon monoxide, a colourless poisonous gas | The excessive brightness of the glowing charcoal | The cooling of the air within the tent | The production of excessive quantities of oxygen
